<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristeidis Stergiadis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristeidis Stergiadis (1861–1949) was a Greek lawyer and administrator, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> as High Commissioner of the Smyrna zone from 1919 to 1922. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, he was known for his stern efforts to treat Muslims and Christians impartially, which made him unpopular with many local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. He left Smyrna as Turkish forces approached in September 1922 and spent the rest of his life in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristeidis-stergiadis.jpg" alt="Aristeidis Stergiadis" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stergiadis,_Paraskevopoulos_and_Pangalos,_Smyrna_October_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stergiadis was born in 1861 in Kandiye, the town now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, which was then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He was named High Commissioner of Smyrna in February 1919 and reached the city four days after the Greek landing of 15 May 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He set to work at once on an administration, on calming the ethnic violence and on plans for the permanent annexation of the Smyrna zone. Greek soldiers held responsible for the violence of 15 and 16 May were court-martialled, and a commission of representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and other allies was set up to decide on payments to the victims.</p><p>His refusal to allow discrimination against the Turkish population was both a conviction and a means of keeping order and good relations with the Allied representatives. Muslims regarded him as a fair governor, and the Ottoman mayor of Smyrna, Osmanzade Hacı Hasan Pasha, called on him to convey their gratitude. The same stance set him against much of the local Greek population, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> and the army, whose troops often ignored his orders. He reportedly carried a stick through the town to beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> who mistreated Turks. On 14 July 1919 the acting foreign secretary urged Venizelos to remove him, but Venizelos kept him in office.</p><p>George Horton recalled a cathedral service at which Stergiadis cut short Archbishop Chrysostomos of Smyrna when the sermon turned political. In 1921, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> to the Turks under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, an estimated 6,500 Rûm left the region; those not taken in on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> were sent to Smyrna, where Stergiadis would not allow the refugees to land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stergiadis left Smyrna on 25 September 1922 aboard a British ship bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and died there on 22 June 1949. His former residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a> houses the Public Archives and Library of Nicolas Kitsikis. He was the stepfather of Beata Kitsikis, mother of Dimitri Kitsikis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristeidis Stergiadis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The High Commissioner <b>Aristeidis Stergiadis</b> ran the zone and tried, with mixed success, to treat Muslims and Christians equally.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna-zone">Smyrna Zone</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.34,25.13&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Heraklion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aristeidis Stergiadis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4661 4537h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4661 4537h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.34,25.13&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
